VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-044
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe swelling of the face and neck, accompanied by rapidly worsening breathlessness and a changing cough, requiring immediate life-saving assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an older patient with a heavy smoking history who awoke with profound facial swelling, distended neck veins, and severe dyspnea. Urgent assessment was required to diagnose an acute Superior Vena Cava (SVC) obstruction, likely secondary to a rapidly growing central malignancy, before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "When I woke up this morning my face was completely puffed up, my neck feels tight, and I can't catch a breath when I lie down."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been progressing rapidly over the past 4 weeks. The patient reports an intractable, changing, new-onset cough and intermittent hemoptysis. There is unexplained, rapid weight loss and profound cachexia. Today, they developed classic Superior Vena Cava (SVC) syndrome, presenting with acute facial swelling and a feeling of fullness in the head that worsens when bending forward. The patient also complains of profound weakness and recent episodes of confusion, potentially indicative of a paraneoplastic syndrome (e.g., SIADH causing hyponatremia). The dyspnea has become rapidly progressive and severe.
Functional Status: Acutely deteriorated; unable to lie flat due to severe orthopnea and head pressure.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of airway compromise or cerebral edema; heavy smoking history.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 26 breaths/min | SpO2: 92% on room air | Wt: 60 Kg (Down 8Kg in 1 month)
General: Highly distressed, cachectic. The face, neck, and upper chest are visibly edematous and plethoric. Veins on the anterior chest wall and neck are markedly dilated and non-pulsatile.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals localized wheezing on the right side, indicating central airway obstruction, but generally decreased air entry globally. No coarse crackles of heart failure. Neurological exam reveals mild confusion and lethargy. The physical presentation of SVC obstruction combined with rapid weight loss and central wheeze points overwhelmingly to a bulky mediastinal mass, most commonly Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC).
Environmental Observations: Patient is sitting bolt upright in a chair, refusing to move to the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications providing relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute SVC Syndrome)
Differentials:

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The edema is strictly localized to the upper body/face, with sparing of the lower extremities, and there are no bibasilar crackles.

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of urticaria, lip/tongue angioedema, or known allergen exposure, and the presence of prominent dilated chest veins and a chronic 4-week cough history.

Deep Vein Thrombosis (Upper Extremity) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The bilateral, symmetrical nature of the facial/neck swelling and the overwhelming respiratory symptoms pointing to a central mediastinal obstructive mass.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Critical

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is presenting with acute Superior Vena Cava (SVC) syndrome, characterized by facial/neck edema, plethora, and dilated superficial collateral veins, caused by external compression of the SVC. Given the rapid 4-week progression of weight loss, changing cough, and localized wheeze, this is almost certainly due to a rapidly dividing central mediastinal tumor, classically Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC). The acute swelling implies compromised venous return from the head, carrying an imminent risk of cerebral edema and lethal airway compromise. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period would predictably result in death. An urgent after-hours home visit was absolutely required to recognize the oncologic emergency, administer high-dose systemic steroids to rapidly reduce tumor-associated edema, and immediately transport the patient to the hospital for emergency stenting or radiation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered an immediate, stat high dose of oral Dexamethasone (16mg) to acutely reduce the edema surrounding the suspected central mass. Activated EMS (000) for a Priority 1 ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department.
Positive Behavioral Change: Maintain a strictly upright, seated posture while waiting for the ambulance; keeping your head elevated above your heart uses gravity to physically drain the trapped blood down from your head and neck, directly reducing the dangerous swelling and pressure in your brain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable; emergency hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the responding intensive care paramedics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is being transferred via emergency ambulance immediately. The situation is a critical oncologic emergency requiring inpatient scanning and intervention.

